<setup>
  <selectFromSequence name="k" from="2" to="4" step="1"/>  
  <selectFromSequence name="a" from="1" to="3" step="1" exclude="$k"/>  
 
  <number name="n">5</number>
  <math name="b" simplify>$a + 2*$n</math>
  <math name="dx" simplify>2</math>

  <function name="f">$k/x</function>

  <math name="L5">$dx * ($$f($a) + $$f($a+$dx) + $$f($a+2*$dx) + $$f($a+3*$dx) + $$f($a+4*$dx) )</math>
  <math name="R5">$dx * ($$f($a+$dx) + $$f($a+2*$dx) + $$f($a+3*$dx) + $$f($a+4*$dx) + $$f($a+5*$dx) )</math>
  <math name="M5">$dx * ($$f($a+$dx/2) + $$f($a+3*$dx/2) + $$f($a+5*$dx/2) + $$f($a+7*$dx/2) + $$f($a+9*$dx/2) )</math>
  <math name="T5">($L5 + $R5)/2</math>
  <math name="S10">(2*$M5 + $T5)/3</math>
  <math simplify name="exactInt">$k*ln($b) - $k*ln($a) </math>

</setup>
<!--
<graph xmin="-5" xmax="$m+20" ymin="-2" ymax="24">
  $c
</graph>
-->
  <p>
    Consider the definite integral 
    <me>
      \int_$a^{$b} \frac{$k}{x} \, dx
    </me>
  </p> 

<p>
  <ol marker="a.">
    <li>
      <p>
        Determine the estimates <m>L_5</m>, <m>R_5</m>, <m>T_5</m>, and <m>M_5</m> for the given definite integral.  (Remember that you can use the Riemann sum calculator provided in the Preview Activity for this section to find <m>L_5</m>, <m>R_5</m>, and <m>M_5</m>.)
      </p>
      <p>
        <m> \int_$a^{$b} \frac{$k}{x} \, dx \approx L_5 = </m><answer allowedErrorInNumbers="0.0001" allowedErrorIsAbsolute> $L5</answer>
      </p>
      <p>
        <m> \int_$a^{$b} \frac{$k}{x} \, dx \approx R_5 = </m><answer allowedErrorInNumbers="0.0001" allowedErrorIsAbsolute> $R5</answer>
      </p>
      <p>
        <m> \int_$a^{$b} \frac{$k}{x} \, dx \approx M_5 = </m><answer allowedErrorInNumbers="0.0001" allowedErrorIsAbsolute> $M5</answer>
      </p>
      <p>
        <m> \int_$a^{$b} \frac{$k}{x} \, dx \approx T_5 = </m><answer allowedErrorInNumbers="0.0001" allowedErrorIsAbsolute> $T5</answer>
      </p>
    </li>  
    <li>
      <p>
        Find the estimate that results from using Simpson's Rule with <m>10</m> subintervals.
      </p>
      <p>
        <m> \int_$a^{$b} \frac{$k}{x} \, dx \approx S_{10} = </m><answer allowedErrorInNumbers="0.0001" allowedErrorIsAbsolute> $S10</answer>
      </p>
    </li>
    <li>
      <p>
        The exact value of the integral turns out to be 
        <me>
          \int_$a^{$b} \frac{$k}{x} \, dx = <math simplify>$k*ln($b) - $k*ln($a) 
          </math>
        </me>
        Simpson's Rule therefore <choiceInput name="c1" inline>
          <choice>?</choice>
          <choice>underestimates</choice>
          <choice>overestimates</choice>
          <choice>exactly matches</choice>
        </choiceInput> the value of the definite integral.
      </p>
      <p>
        <answer><award><when>
          ($c1.selectedIndices=2 and $exactInt>$S10)
          or
          ($c1.selectedIndices=3 and $S10>$exactInt)
          or
          ($c1.selectedIndices=4 and $S10=$exactInt)
        </when></award></answer>
      </p>
          
    </li>

  </ol>  
</p>